{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["And the sense of <b>And to the elders he said, Wait for us here</b> is that when he parted from them with his attendant, he commanded them to wait for him there. But the meaning is not that they would stand there all day and all night until his return, for he said <b>Behold, Aaron and Hur are with you; whoever has a matter, let him come to them</b>, and those who have matters would be in the camp, and there is the place of judgment, and he had already said to all of them, \"Return to your tents\" (Deuteronomy 5:27). Rather, the meaning is that they would stand and come as far as that place, and would not presume to ascend after them, even to the place of Joshua, until his return to them.\n\nAnd in my opinion it is possible to interpret \"wait\" as \"wait for us in our stead and in our place in the camp, and Aaron and Hur are with you,\" and \"whoever has a matter\" — from the difficult case that they would bring to me — \"let him come to them\" in my stead. And he said \"to them\" as an expression of elevation for Aaron and Hur, for before all of them he would command them to come, and they would all be in one station, as he said \"with you.\" So he commanded that the elders and Aaron and Hur sit as the seat of a court, like Moses over the officers of thousands and hundreds, until his return, for he knew that he would be delayed on the mountain. And he said \"for us\" as an expression of honor toward the student, as he said, \"Choose for us men\" (Exodus 17:9). And this is the correct interpretation.\n\nBut Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 24:14): \"And to the elders he said\" — at his departure from the camp — \"wait for us here\" — remain there with the rest of the people, to be ready to judge every man's dispute. And this is not possible, for they were not now in the camp, and what sense would there be in his saying so to them while they were sitting in the camp, when judges had already been appointed?"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ב\"ד is resolved as בית דין (a court). The phrase דֶּרֶךְ מַעֲלָה (\"as an expression of elevation\") refers to grammatical or rhetorical elevation, i.e., using the plural \"to them\" as a form of honor."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:15:55.131411434Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}